/*
  Localizable.strings (en)
  VigilUI — base localisation.

  Keys ARE the English text, which is the SwiftUI convention for a base localisation: a
  missing key falls back to the key itself, so English renders correctly whether or not this
  file is present. It exists for two reasons.

  1. It is the file a translator starts from. ru.lproj is this file with the values replaced,
     key for key and section for section, so that the two can be read side by side.
  2. Without at least one real (non-dotfile) resource, SwiftPM emits an EMPTY resource bundle
     with no Info.plist, and codesign then refuses it outright:
       Vigil_VigilUI.bundle: bundle format unrecognized, invalid, or unsuitable
     That failure stopped the very first Mac build at the signing step. A directory holding
     only .placeholder is invisible to SwiftPM, because it skips dotfiles.

  75 keys, covering every LocalizedStringKey in Sources/VigilUI.

  Format specifiers are the ones SwiftUI's LocalizedStringKey interpolation emits, and they
  are part of the key: a String interpolates as %@ and an Int as %lld. Getting one wrong
  does not mistranslate a sentence, it feeds String(format:) the wrong argument size.

  Count-bearing keys are NOT here. They live in Localizable.stringsdict, because English
  cannot say "1 frames" and Russian needs one/few/many plus other where English needs
  one/other. A key must appear in exactly one of the two tables, never both.
*/

/* The connect form — the first screen (UX.md §8.3) */
"Connect to a camera" = "Connect to a camera";
"Vigil needs the camera's address and the password it was set up with. Nothing leaves your network." = "Vigil needs the camera's address and the password it was set up with. Nothing leaves your network.";
"Address" = "Address";
"User name" = "User name";
"Password" = "Password";
"The camera's password" = "The camera's password";
"Connect" = "Connect";
"192.168.1.64" = "192.168.1.64";
"admin" = "admin";

/* Field validation. The row is lineLimit(1) at Caption1 in ~380 pt: keep it short */
"Enter the camera's address." = "Enter the camera's address.";
"Use an IP address like 192.168.1.64, or a host name." = "Use an IP address like 192.168.1.64, or a host name.";

/* Longest message row in the form. The English is already ~74 characters against a
   lineLimit(1) row about 380 pt wide, so the Russian is deliberately shorter rather
   than a literal rendering. VTextField.swift:328-331 sets lineLimit(1) on this row. */
"Enter the account name. Hikvision cameras use admin unless it was changed." = "Enter the account name. Hikvision cameras use admin unless it was changed.";
"Enter the camera's password." = "Enter the camera's password.";

/* Diagnosis titles: the fact, at most six words, ending in a full stop */
"Can't reach this camera." = "Can't reach this camera.";
"Camera isn't activated." = "Camera isn't activated.";
"Wrong password." = "Wrong password.";
"Device is locked." = "Device is locked.";
"Video port is closed." = "Video port is closed.";
"Not a Hikvision device." = "Not a Hikvision device.";
"Unsupported video format." = "Unsupported video format.";
"No video is arriving." = "No video is arriving.";
"The picture has stalled." = "The picture has stalled.";
"Vigil couldn't finish signing in." = "Vigil couldn't finish signing in.";

/* Diagnosis messages: the cause and the next step. R1.5 — never a code, never a dead end */
"No response from %@. Check that it's powered on and on the same network as this Mac." = "No response from %@. Check that it's powered on and on the same network as this Mac.";
"%@ is brand new and has no password yet. Set one now to start using it." = "%@ is brand new and has no password yet. Set one now to start using it.";
"%@ rejected this password. Check it and try again — too many attempts will lock the device." = "%@ rejected this password. Check it and try again — too many attempts will lock the device.";
"%@ locked this account after too many failed sign-ins. It unlocks on its own, or immediately if you reboot the camera." = "%@ locked this account after too many failed sign-ins. It unlocks on its own, or immediately if you reboot the camera.";
"Vigil reached %@ on port %lld, but RTSP port %lld refused the connection." = "Vigil reached %@ on port %lld, but RTSP port %lld refused the connection.";
"%@ answered, but it doesn't speak ISAPI. Vigil can still try ONVIF, with fewer features." = "%@ answered, but it doesn't speak ISAPI. Vigil can still try ONVIF, with fewer features.";
"This camera streams %@. Vigil plays H.265, H.264 and MJPEG." = "This camera streams %@. Vigil plays H.265, H.264 and MJPEG.";
"%@ started the stream but sent no video. Something on the network is blocking UDP." = "%@ started the stream but sent no video. Something on the network is blocking UDP.";
"%@ is sending data but no complete picture yet. Vigil has asked it for a new keyframe." = "%@ is sending data but no complete picture yet. Vigil has asked it for a new keyframe.";
"%@ answered, but not in a way Vigil recognises. The device's own reply is in the details below." = "%@ answered, but not in a way Vigil recognises. The device's own reply is in the details below.";

/* Remedies. Every failure offers at least one (R1.5) */
"Try Again" = "Try Again";
"Check the Address" = "Check the Address";
"Update Password" = "Update Password";
"Set Password" = "Set Password";
"Try Port 8554" = "Try Port 8554";
"Switch to TCP" = "Switch to TCP";
"Use ONVIF" = "Use ONVIF";
"Open Camera Web Page" = "Open Camera Web Page";
"Diagnose" = "Diagnose";
"Details" = "Details";

/* The status word beside the dot. Colour never carries state alone (DESIGN.md §10.5) */
"Connecting" = "Connecting";
"Live" = "Live";
"Degraded" = "Degraded";
"Offline" = "Offline";

/* The connecting narration ladder (UX.md §12.4) */
"Looking up %@…" = "Looking up %@…";
"Connecting…" = "Connecting…";
"Signing in…" = "Signing in…";
"Negotiating stream…" = "Negotiating stream…";
"Opening video channel…" = "Opening video channel…";
"Waiting for video…" = "Waiting for video…";
"Waiting for a keyframe…" = "Waiting for a keyframe…";

/* Measured degradation. The number is formatted by FormatStyle, so it is already localised */
/* The number arrives already localised from FormatStyle: `3.1%` in English and
   `3,1 %` with a space in Russian (UX.md §14.1 rule 9). Never add a percent sign
   here, and never hard-code the separator. */
"%@ packet loss — video may stutter." = "%@ packet loss — video may stutter.";
"%@ of jitter — video may stutter." = "%@ of jitter — video may stutter.";

/* Both counts arrive as pre-formatted strings (%@), so no plural rule can ever apply
   to them. The Russian is therefore phrased around the invariable unit `кадр/с`
   instead of a noun that would have to agree with the number. */
"Showing %@ of %@ frames per second." = "Showing %@ of %@ frames per second.";
"Switched to TCP for stability." = "Switched to TCP for stability.";

/* Offline and reconnecting (UX.md §12.6) */
"Still can't reach this camera." = "Still can't reach this camera.";
"Connection lost." = "Connection lost.";
"Waiting for the network." = "Waiting for the network.";

/* Count-bearing, and still correct in a flat table: `с` is an abbreviation and
   `попытка 3` is a label, so neither inflects with the number. Moving this to the
   stringsdict would invite `попытки 3`, which is wrong. */
"Reconnecting in %lld s (attempt %lld)." = "Reconnecting in %lld s (attempt %lld).";
"Retry Now" = "Retry Now";

/* The stamp is relative under an hour (`4 min ago`) and absolute after (`10:14`), so
   the sentence has to read correctly with both. Russian uses a colon for exactly
   that reason: `Последний кадр: 4 мин. назад` and `Последний кадр: 10:14`. */
"Last seen %@" = "Last seen %@";

/* The connecting overlay */
/* A camera name, not an address. UX.md §14.2 asks for locale-correct quotes around
   an inserted name, and the quotes belong in the translation rather than in Swift —
   which is also what keeps the name in a nominative-safe position. */
"Connecting to %@" = "Connecting to %@";

/* The unit is inside the key because Measurement's abbreviated width spells seconds
   `sec` and its narrow width drops the space. Russian uses `с` (GOST 8.417). */
"%@ s" = "%@ s";
"This is taking longer than usual." = "This is taking longer than usual.";

/* Components */
"Hide password" = "Hide password";
"Show password" = "Show password";
"Loading" = "Loading";

/* Only in #if DEBUG previews today. docs/UX.md §14.3–§14.4 names the first three as
   shipping copy, so they are translated in advance; an unused key costs nothing. */
"Add Manually" = "Add Manually";
"Delete Camera" = "Delete Camera";
"Snapshot" = "Snapshot";
"Required" = "Required";

/* Ruled in docs/RULING-LOCKOUT.md §3 but not yet in ConnectDiagnosis.swift: the
   signInPaused diagnosis, where Vigil itself is the one holding back. Wording quoted from
   the ruling. Inert until the case lands — if the literal differs the key simply misses. */
"Waiting before trying again" = "Waiting before trying again";
